> 1. **A (depth $L\ge3$, cube, $c=1$): correct after repairs.** The memorizer must be re-derived for real values and accuracy $a$ (Lemma 2.1), which adds an energy term $9(H+1)/(2a)$ that the draft omits. Theorem 2.2 is the corrected statement. Its constants are confirmed: $\beta(1)=0.484829$, and $\max_\lambda\beta=0.517351$ at $\lambda=1.5029$. **Main caveat:** the failure needs learner width $N\ge N_0=\Theta(d^2n/a^2)$. At learner widths with $N(N+d)\log n=o(n)$ and input dimension $d=o(\sqrt n)$, for example $N\le n^{1/2-\epsilon}$ with $d\le N$, the same learner with clipped predictions is agnostically consistent against every fixed competitor of the repository architecture with width at most $N$, by parameter counting (`small-learner-width.md`, Theorems 1 and 4, Remark 8). A therefore refutes the width-uniform conjecture, which is the brief's formalisation. It does not refute a version in which the learner's width is a fixed small power of $n$. [At finalization: Claim A is proved in [`A-tight-conditioning-fails.md`](A-tight-conditioning-fails.md) Theorem 4.4, with Corollaries 4.5 and 4.5a, using a memorizer with a smaller first-layer energy (its Lemma 2.1). There the failure holds at learner widths $N\ge N_0=O(n\log^2n/a^2)$ for a fixed accuracy $a$, with certified excess tending to $(\sqrt{\beta(\lambda)}-a\sqrt\lambda)_+^2$, and at $N\ge N_0=O(n\log^4n)$ for the full excess $\beta(\lambda)-o(1)$. In the terms of that note (its §0.1 and Proposition 5.1), Claim A refutes the width-uniform property (P$_L$) of the brief for one learner, SP$_c$ with $cK_{\rm mem}=o(n)$, which includes $c=1$, on laws whose input dimension $d=\log_2n$ grows with $n$. It does not refute the conjecture, which lets the learner be chosen: [`B-tempered-likelihood.md`](B-tempered-likelihood.md) proves (P$_L$) for the tempered SP$_c$ with clipped predictions. The sentence "A therefore refutes the width-uniform conjecture" is to be read in this sense. The small-width result is proved in [`small-learner-width.md`](small-learner-width.md).]
> 2. **A′ (continuous inputs, deterministic labels): correct** (Proposition 2.4). The precise condition is that each cluster's radius is below $n^{-2}$ divided by the learner's Lipschitz bound. That bound is $(\kappa_N/L)^{L/2}/\sqrt d$ (Lemma 1.0, the AM–GM form). [At finalization: Claim A′ is proved in [`A-tight-conditioning-fails.md`](A-tight-conditioning-fails.md) Theorem 7.2, with Proposition 7.2a and Corollary 7.2b, on a different law: small corner cubes at the vertices of $\{-1,1\}^d$, with a deterministic, balanced label. That note adds that such a law is near-atomic at the learner's resolution, with density $2^{\Theta(d^2)}$ times uniform, and that input laws with density ratio $2^{o(d^2)}$ and noisy labels are open (its §0.1, Proposition 7.4 and Remark 7.5).]
> 3. **B (tempered $c=n^{1-\theta}$): correct, with a better exponent.** For unclipped outputs the draft's bookkeeping through the energy cover does not work; that route gives only $\theta<1/(4L+2)$. The path-norm Rademacher bound already in the repository (`NNG/works-in-progress/noisy-interpolation/README.md`, eq. (15)) gives B for all $\theta<1/H$ with clipped outputs and all $\theta<1/L$ with unclipped outputs (Theorem 3.2, Corollary 3.3). The rate is $\tilde O(n^{-1/(H+2)})$. [At finalization: proved in [`B-tempered-likelihood.md`](B-tempered-likelihood.md), against competitors of the repository architecture with the learner's depth, at every learner width $N\ge m$ and with explicit polynomial sample sizes: Theorem 6.3 for clipped predictions and every $\theta<1/H$, with excess $\tilde O(\mathrm{poly}(d,m,B)\,n^{-1/(H+2)})$ at $\theta=1/(H+2)$, and Theorem 6.4 for unclipped predictions and every $\theta<1/L$. The posterior mean of Theorem 6.3 is the mean of the clipped outputs; the Bayes model average and its clip are covered only for $\theta<1/L$ (B §0).]
